[{"id":"gemstones-amethyst","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Amethyst","slug":"amethyst","summary":"Purple quartz belongs to the same mineral species as citrine. Colour zoning and crystal form can be interesting features, but neither establishes natural origin or treatment status.","family":"Quartz","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["purple"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Quartz"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"7"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Purple quartz belongs to the same mineral species as citrine. Colour zoning and crystal form can be interesting features, but neither establishes natural origin or treatment status.\n\nAmethyst may be heated to alter its colour. Do not infer an untreated history from a deep purple photograph."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Keep prolonged intense light and heat out of the storage environment; ask for care advice that reflects any treatment."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Compare purple amethyst with yellow citrine as varieties of quartz, rather than as different mineral species. A stone showing purple and yellow zones may be described as ametrine, but a two-colour photograph does not settle its identity. Look at the distribution of colour through the whole stone under ordinary illumination. Reflections from a coloured backing or a strong photographic light can exaggerate the impression. For a cut stone, ask for views from the face, side and back so that a discussion about zoning refers to the same object."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"A rough crystal cluster and a faceted amethyst answer different visual questions. The cluster illustrates growth and surface form; the cut stone illustrates how a cutter used the material. Do not compare their apparent clarity as though both were transparent finished gems. When considering a setting, ask about exposed points, existing fractures and cleaning. Keep amethyst away from harder stones in storage. If colour is the main reason for a choice, ask whether the photographs were corrected and request a neutral-light view."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For amethyst, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Amethyst reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/amethyst","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["quartz","citrine"],"seoTitle":"Amethyst: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore amethyst, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-citrine","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Citrine","slug":"citrine","summary":"Citrine is yellow to orange quartz. Its relationship to amethyst is mineralogical, rather than a statement that every yellow quartz crystal formed with its present colour.","family":"Quartz","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["yellow"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Quartz"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"7"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Citrine is yellow to orange quartz. Its relationship to amethyst is mineralogical, rather than a statement that every yellow quartz crystal formed with its present colour.\n\nMuch commercial citrine is produced by heating amethyst. A natural quartz identification and an untreated colour claim are different statements."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Request a written colour-treatment description, especially when comparing a pale yellow stone with strongly orange material."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"The useful comparison is with amethyst and other yellow materials, not with a generic category of precious yellow stones. Quartz and topaz are different minerals even when both look yellow. A seller should state the material first and describe colour and treatment separately. Ask whether the colour description concerns the present appearance or claims an untreated history. An attractive commercial name is not a substitute for that distinction. Compare like-sized stones in the same lighting before drawing conclusions about saturation or apparent brightness."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"A photograph of a citrine crystal should not be taken as evidence that all citrine reaches the market in that form or colour. Rough material can have uneven colour, broken surfaces and opaque areas that are not represented in a finished gem. For jewellery, discuss how much of the surface will be exposed and whether the stone has fractures or coatings. Keep the written description with the item. When evidence about colour treatment is unavailable, record it as unknown rather than converting a quartz identification into an untreated claim."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For citrine, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Citrine reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/citrine","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["quartz","amethyst"],"seoTitle":"Citrine: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore citrine, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-peridot","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Peridot","slug":"peridot","summary":"Peridot is the gem variety of olivine. Its yellowish to brownish green range differs from the many colours possible in some other gem families.","family":"Olivine","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["green"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Olivine"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"6.5–7"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Peridot is the gem variety of olivine. Its yellowish to brownish green range differs from the many colours possible in some other gem families.\n\nInternal doubling can be visible in this doubly refractive material. It is an observation for examination, not a sufficient home identification test."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Protect exposed edges from knocks and avoid acids and abrupt temperature changes. Do not use a scratch test on a finished gem."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Compare peridot with green tourmaline or emerald by material identity before comparing shade. Different minerals can produce a similar green impression in a small photograph. In peridot, an examiner may observe doubling of features through the stone, but this depends on viewing direction and cannot be used as a universal photograph-based test. Ask which observations support an identification and whether an independent report was issued. A geographical story should be supported separately; the green colour alone does not show where the crystal formed."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"For a ring or bracelet, exposed edges and accidental impact are practical concerns. Ask a jeweller to inspect the setting and visible fractures before deciding how the item will be worn. A pendant may face different knocks from a ring, but setting design and the individual stone still matter. Store peridot separately and keep a record of the care advice supplied. If cleaning history is uncertain, stop before experimenting with household acids or mechanical cleaners. A cautious professional assessment is more useful than a damaging home identification experiment."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For peridot, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Peridot reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/peridot","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Peridot: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore peridot, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-topaz","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Topaz","slug":"topaz","summary":"Topaz is a separate mineral species, not a general name for yellow gems. It occurs in several colours; commercial blue topaz commonly owes its colour to treatment.","family":"Topaz","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["blue"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Topaz"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"8"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Topaz is a separate mineral species, not a general name for yellow gems. It occurs in several colours; commercial blue topaz commonly owes its colour to treatment.\n\nAn appealing trade colour name is not a laboratory conclusion. Ask whether irradiation, heating or coating is involved in the particular stone."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Topaz has cleavage. Its Mohs hardness does not remove the risk of splitting after a blow; discuss protective settings and cleaning with a professional."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Compare a blue topaz description with aquamarine carefully. A similar pale blue colour does not make them the same mineral, and a photograph cannot supply the distinction. Likewise, a yellow stone called topaz should still have an identification rather than rely on an old commercial colour association. Ask for the material name, colour description, known treatment and any coating to appear together in the written record. If a colour term implies an origin or exceptional rarity, ask what evidence supports that additional statement."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"When planning jewellery, cleavage deserves its own discussion. Resistance to scratching does not tell you how a crystal may respond to a concentrated blow. Ask whether the setting protects vulnerable edges and whether the stone has existing damage near the girdle or corners. Before repair or resetting, tell the jeweller what is known about treatment. A coated stone may require different handling from an uncoated one. Keep any report and seller disclosure with the item rather than assuming future cleaners will recognize its treatment by appearance."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For topaz, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Topaz reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/topaz","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Topaz: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore topaz, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-spinel","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Spinel","slug":"spinel","summary":"Spinel is distinct from corundum, even though red spinel and ruby can look similar. Red, pink, blue and other colours occur.","family":"Spinel","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["pink"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Spinel"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"8"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Spinel is distinct from corundum, even though red spinel and ruby can look similar. Red, pink, blue and other colours occur.\n\nA historic association with ruby does not make spinel a ruby variety. Laboratory-grown spinel also exists; material and formation must be stated separately."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Assess the actual stone for fractures and treatment before cleaning. A family name alone cannot describe its condition."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"A useful comparison is red spinel and ruby. Their similar appearance explains why a clear material identification matters, but modern descriptions should not merge them into one mineral. Another comparison is natural and laboratory-grown spinel: a shared mineral name is different from a conclusion about formation. Ask which of those questions the report addresses. A colour photograph, including a very vivid one, is not an identification record. Check that the photographed object, measurements and report details can be matched without relying only on a familiar colour."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Assess colour under more than one ordinary light and ask about the actual condition of the stone. Visible inclusions or a chipped edge may affect a setting decision independently of the mineral name. Spinel hardness does not eliminate the need to protect an exposed gem. For an existing piece, inspect the mounting as well as the stone before cleaning. If an advertised origin or treatment claim is important to your decision, retain the supporting conclusion in writing and distinguish it from the seller’s general description of the material."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For spinel, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Spinel reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/spinel","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Spinel: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore spinel, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-garnet","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Garnet","slug":"garnet","summary":"Garnet is a mineral group rather than one uniform species. Pyrope, almandine, spessartine, grossular and andradite are examples within its broad gem vocabulary.","family":"Garnet","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["red"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Garnet"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"6.5–7.5"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Garnet is a mineral group rather than one uniform species. Pyrope, almandine, spessartine, grossular and andradite are examples within its broad gem vocabulary.\n\nRed is familiar, but garnets also occur in other colours. Refractive index and density vary with composition, so one number should not be assigned to every garnet."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Ask which species or variety is being described. Compare colour, inclusions and cut for the individual gem, with treatment and condition recorded."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Begin a garnet comparison with the species or variety, where it has been established. A red almandine-rich gem, an orange spessartine and a green grossular need not have identical optical or density values. The group name connects related materials without collapsing their differences. A trade variety name may describe colour or a familiar composition, but it should not be treated as a universal quality grade. Ask how the material was identified and whether a numerical property in a description is a measured result or a general reference range."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"For an individual stone, compare face-up colour, visible inclusions and cut in a consistent viewing setup. Dark areas in a photograph may reflect the lighting or the way light travels through a particular cut. They should not be interpreted as a chemical analysis. Ask a jeweller about the combination of condition, mounting and intended wear before selecting a design. Keep any species, variety and treatment statements together. When the evidence supports only the broader group name, it is more accurate to retain that scope than to guess a specific member."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For garnet, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Garnet reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/garnet","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Garnet: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore garnet, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-tourmaline","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Tourmaline","slug":"tourmaline","summary":"Tourmaline is a group of minerals with many colours and compositions. A single crystal may contain distinct colour zones.","family":"Tourmaline","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["multicolour"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Tourmaline"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"7–7.5"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Tourmaline is a group of minerals with many colours and compositions. A single crystal may contain distinct colour zones.\n\nNames such as rubellite and indicolite describe familiar trade colour categories. They do not, by themselves, establish species, geographical origin or treatment."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Heat, filling and other enhancement questions require disclosure for the particular stone. Avoid equating a vivid photograph with a complete report."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"A multicoloured crystal can make the tourmaline group particularly interesting to explore. Its visible zones may be used by a cutter to create a two-colour or watermelon appearance, but the resulting outline does not determine mineral species or mine. Compare a colour term such as rubellite with the actual identification on a report. If a special trade designation is used, ask what conditions the designation refers to and whether they were examined. Colour, chemistry, geographical origin and treatment are related questions, but none should silently replace the others."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"For cut stones, ask to see the same gem from more than one direction and in neutral lighting. Orientation and cutting can influence how dark or bright a colour appears. Consider the intended setting with a jeweller rather than choosing only from a highly lit close-up. Keep treatment information with the stone, including any disclosure concerning clarity enhancement. A reference photograph of one tourmaline should not stand for every member of this diverse group. If a claim goes beyond what a report examined, ask for the scope to be made explicit."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For tourmaline, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Tourmaline reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/tourmaline","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Tourmaline: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore tourmaline, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-zircon","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Zircon","slug":"zircon","summary":"Zircon is a naturally occurring mineral and is different from cubic zirconia. Confusing their names obscures a fundamental difference in material identity.","family":"Zircon","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["blue"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Zircon"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"6–7.5"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Zircon is a naturally occurring mineral and is different from cubic zirconia. Confusing their names obscures a fundamental difference in material identity.\n\nZircon properties vary with structural condition. Some material shows conspicuous doubling; heating can modify colour. Neither observation alone proves an origin."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Protect facet edges from abrasion and knocks. Ask the seller for a clear material name rather than an abbreviation that could be confused with CZ."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Read zircon and cubic zirconia as different material names. A shortened label can create confusion, especially in a mixed jewellery description. Ask the seller to spell out the identification and to separate it from colour, treatment and formation statements. General zircon reference values also need context because structural condition affects its properties. An observation of doubled features may be relevant to an examination, but a single blurred or reflected line in a photograph is not a reliable test. Avoid identifying a stone solely by comparing it with a web image."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"When comparing finished gems, inspect the condition of facet junctions and exposed edges as well as their colour. Discuss protection and storage with the jeweller, particularly for an item that may knock against other jewellery. Cleaning advice should take the actual stone and mounting into account. Keep the identification and any heating disclosure together in the record. A familiar blue colour does not certify a treatment history, and a reference range is not an analysis of your specimen. Seek clarification when the written description changes between zircon and a simulant name."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For zircon, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Zircon reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/zircon","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Zircon: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore zircon, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-tanzanite","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Tanzanite","slug":"tanzanite","summary":"Tanzanite is the blue to violet gem variety of zoisite. Different viewing directions and lighting can change the colour impression.","family":"Zoisite","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["violet blue"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Zoisite"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"6–7"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Tanzanite is the blue to violet gem variety of zoisite. Different viewing directions and lighting can change the colour impression.\n\nHeating is common in the trade. The name does not certify that a particular stone is unheated or support an unqualified rarity or investment claim."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Cleavage and sensitivity to sudden temperature changes matter when setting and cleaning. Seek specific advice before using mechanical cleaners."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"A comparison with blue sapphire should begin with the fact that tanzanite is zoisite and sapphire is corundum. A shared blue or violet impression does not make their durability or care the same. View the same tanzanite under different ordinary lights and from more than one direction. Ask whether the photographs use a consistent white balance, because colour comparisons can otherwise become comparisons of camera settings. The written record should identify the material and state what is known about heating, rather than imply an unheated history from a colour alone."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Discuss jewellery design before deciding how frequently an exposed stone will be worn. Cleavage and the risk of impact matter even when a gem looks clear and bright. A protective mounting may be appropriate, but the individual stone and setting need professional assessment. Before cleaning or repair, pass along treatment and condition information. Do not use a hardness test on a finished tanzanite. The practical question is how to care for this particular piece, not whether a reference number can be used as a promise against every kind of damage."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For tanzanite, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Tanzanite reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/tanzanite","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Tanzanite: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore tanzanite, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-opal","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Opal","slug":"opal","summary":"Opal is hydrated silica. Precious opal displays play-of-colour, while common opal does not; body colour and play-of-colour are separate observations.","family":"Hydrated silica","material":"Mineraloid","colours":["multicolour"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Hydrated silica"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"5–6.5"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Opal is hydrated silica. Precious opal displays play-of-colour, while common opal does not; body colour and play-of-colour are separate observations.\n\nA solid opal is different in construction from an opal doublet or triplet. Request a description of the entire object, including backing or protective layers."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Avoid heat and sudden environmental changes. Ask about water sensitivity and any impregnation or assembled construction before choosing a cleaning method."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Separate body colour, play-of-colour and construction when reading an opal description. Two photographs may capture quite different flashes from the same precious opal as the angle changes. A short video can help explain that movement, but it does not prove that the object is solid opal or untreated. Ask for views that show the side and back where appropriate. A doublet or triplet includes assembled components, and those components belong in the description. Common opal can be attractive without play-of-colour; its absence is not automatically evidence of an imitation."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Plan care around the individual material and the way it has been assembled. Ask about porosity, treatments and any backing or protective layer before immersing a piece or choosing a cleaner. Storage advice should reflect the actual opal rather than a universal internet recipe. Avoid heat and abrupt changes, and ask a professional to assess cracking or an uncertain surface before repair. Keep construction information with the item. A photograph selected to illustrate play-of-colour is an educational example, not a statement that all opals show that effect or tolerate identical handling."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For opal, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Opal reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/opal","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Opal: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore opal, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-moonstone","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Moonstone","slug":"moonstone","summary":"Moonstone belongs to the feldspar family. Its characteristic moving sheen is called adularescence and is associated with its internal structure.","family":"Feldspar","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["white"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Feldspar"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"6–6.5"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Moonstone belongs to the feldspar family. Its characteristic moving sheen is called adularescence and is associated with its internal structure.\n\nA blue sheen is an appearance observation, not proof of origin or identity. Trade labels may cover different feldspar materials, so request the actual identification."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Protect cabochon surfaces and avoid ultrasonic and steam cleaning. Inspect the setting and any fractures before adopting a cleaning routine."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"To understand adularescence, look for a sheen that moves across the gem as the light or viewing direction changes. A still photograph may capture only one part of that appearance. Ask for a neutral-light video if the effect matters to your choice, while remembering that video still does not establish material identity. A trade colour name should be read alongside the actual feldspar identification. Compare moonstone with sunstone as different gem appearances in a broad family, rather than assuming that their light effects are interchangeable."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"A smooth cabochon can make the moving sheen easy to observe, but its polished surface still needs protection. Ask about existing fractures and how the mounting supports the stone. Do not treat the mineral-family label as a guarantee that every item can be cleaned mechanically. Discuss a gentle cleaning method and the condition of the setting with a jeweller. A photograph with a blue reflection should not be described as proof of a particular source country. Keep any origin assertion separate from the visible appearance and identification evidence."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For moonstone, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Moonstone reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/moonstone","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["feldspar","sunstone"],"seoTitle":"Moonstone: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore moonstone, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-alexandrite","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Alexandrite","slug":"alexandrite","summary":"Alexandrite is a colour-change variety of chrysoberyl. Its appearance differs under different types of illumination, rather than simply changing with the camera angle.","family":"Chrysoberyl","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["colour change"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Chrysoberyl"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"8.5"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Alexandrite is a colour-change variety of chrysoberyl. Its appearance differs under different types of illumination, rather than simply changing with the camera angle.\n\nOther natural and synthetic materials can also show colour change. A green-to-red video does not establish that a gem is natural alexandrite."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Ask for comparable views under stated lighting and an appropriate identification report. Do not infer geographic origin or treatment from the strength of the change."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"A useful colour-change comparison requires stated illumination. Compare the same object in the two lighting conditions, with camera settings and backgrounds described as consistently as possible. Do not confuse a change with viewing direction, a coloured reflection or an edited photograph with the relevant colour-change observation. Even a persuasive comparison does not prove natural origin: other materials and laboratory-grown counterparts must be considered by an appropriate examination. Ask what the report identifies and whether the photographed object can be matched to that report."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"When deciding on a piece of jewellery, consider the colour you will actually see in the places where it is worn. A dramatic side-by-side photograph can be informative, but it should not replace an ordinary viewing experience. Ask about inclusions, condition and any treatment before setting or cleaning. Keep an origin conclusion only when it is supported and within the report’s scope. A famous historical source does not establish where a particular modern stone formed. Document uncertainty rather than attaching an attractive origin story to colour change alone."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For alexandrite, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Alexandrite reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/alexandrite","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Alexandrite: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore alexandrite, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-diamond","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Diamond","slug":"diamond","summary":"Diamond is crystalline carbon. Natural and laboratory-grown diamonds must be distinguished in descriptions; simulants are different materials that resemble diamond.","family":"Diamond","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["colourless"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Diamond"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"10"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Diamond is crystalline carbon. Natural and laboratory-grown diamonds must be distinguished in descriptions; simulants are different materials that resemble diamond.\n\nHardness describes resistance to scratching. Diamond can still chip or cleave after a blow, so the highest Mohs number is not a promise of indestructibility."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Read the report scope and comments, and check how the report is matched to the stone. A grading report for another diamond is not transferable evidence."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Separate identification, formation and grading. A report identifying a diamond does not automatically answer every grading question, while a laboratory-grown diamond is different from a diamond simulant. Read the exact report type and the conclusions it contains. When comparing colour and clarity grades, check that the reports use the relevant grading system and that the documents match the stones. An online report lookup confirms a record, but matching that record to an object remains a separate step. Measurements, inscriptions where present and a professional examination may help with that process."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Consider wear and setting independently of the Mohs scale. A diamond may resist scratching while remaining vulnerable to chipping from an impact, particularly at an exposed point or existing weakness. Ask a jeweller to inspect the stone and mounting before repair or intensive cleaning. Treatments and surface coatings require disclosure and can change the care discussion. A photograph alone cannot resolve all these details. Keep the full report and written description, including comments, so that a future repairer can assess the actual item rather than rely on the general reputation of diamond."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For diamond, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Diamond reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/diamond","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Diamond: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore diamond, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-morganite","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Morganite","slug":"morganite","summary":"Morganite is pink to orange-pink beryl. It shares its mineral family with emerald and aquamarine, but their colour names are not interchangeable.","family":"Beryl","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["pink"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Beryl"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"7.5–8"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Morganite is pink to orange-pink beryl. It shares its mineral family with emerald and aquamarine, but their colour names are not interchangeable.\n\nPale colour can be difficult to compare across different lighting and backgrounds. Heating may affect appearance; ask what is known about the individual gem."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Examine visible inclusions and setting protection rather than assuming all beryl needs identical care. Store jewellery separately to reduce abrasion."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Compare morganite with aquamarine and emerald as members of beryl, while keeping their colour varieties distinct. Pink or orange-pink appearance is an introduction to the subject, not a substitute for identifying a particular stone. In pale gems, background, lighting and viewing distance can strongly affect a photograph’s colour impression. Ask for comparable views when deciding between stones. A larger close-up should not be mistaken for the appearance of a small finished gem at ordinary distance. Keep a claim about heating separate from the material name."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Discuss the intended setting in relation to visible inclusions and the way the stone will be exposed to wear. A reference hardness range does not measure the condition of an individual morganite. Ask about any existing fractures, previous repair or enhancement before resetting a piece. Store it separately from harder jewellery and obtain cleaning instructions appropriate to the mounting. If the available evidence only identifies beryl and its colour variety, do not convert that into an unsupported statement about geographic origin or an untreated colour history."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For morganite, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Morganite reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/morganite","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["beryl","emerald","aquamarine"],"seoTitle":"Morganite: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore morganite, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-jade","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Jade","slug":"jade","summary":"Jade is an umbrella gem name that includes jadeite and nephrite, rather than a single mineral with one fixed set of properties. Certain green omphacite materials are also included in gemological usage.","family":"Jade materials","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["green"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Jade materials"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"varies by material"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Jade is an umbrella gem name that includes jadeite and nephrite, rather than a single mineral with one fixed set of properties. Certain green omphacite materials are also included in gemological usage.\n\nColour alone cannot separate these materials or establish whether jade has been dyed, impregnated or otherwise treated. Ask for the actual material and treatment conclusion."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Avoid applying one hardness or cleaning rule to all jade objects. Surface treatments and assembled pieces need individual assessment."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Begin with the actual material rather than a single green colour category. Jadeite and nephrite differ mineralogically, and the broad gem term jade should not be used to assign one fixed density, refractive index or hardness to every object. An opaque carving may require a different examination approach from a small translucent cabochon. Ask which material was established and what was assessed for treatment. A familiar commercial grade or colour name is not a replacement for the underlying identification and disclosure."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"For a carved object, inspect the entire piece rather than only a bright patch in a close-up. Surface polish, fractures, thin edges and any assembled components affect handling. Ask about dye, polymer impregnation and other treatments in precise terms, and retain the answers in writing. Cleaning advice must reflect those details. A matrix or colour pattern should not be used as proof of a source location. If a report describes only part of an assembled object, clarify which components were examined before treating the conclusion as applying to the whole piece."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For jade, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Jade reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/jade","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Jade: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore jade, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-lapis-lazuli","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Lapis lazuli","slug":"lapis-lazuli","summary":"Lapis lazuli is a rock made of several minerals, with lazurite responsible for its characteristic blue. Calcite and pyrite can be visible components.","family":"Rock aggregate","material":"Rock aggregate","colours":["blue"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Rock aggregate"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"varies by composition"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Lapis lazuli is a rock made of several minerals, with lazurite responsible for its characteristic blue. Calcite and pyrite can be visible components.\n\nA rock aggregate should not be assigned the single optical constants of a pure mineral species. Dyed material and imitations also need to be distinguished."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Keep acids, aggressive chemicals and heat away. Ask about dye, wax and other surface treatments before cleaning a carving or bead strand."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Look at lapis lazuli as an aggregate. Blue areas, pale calcite and metallic-looking pyrite can contribute to its visible character, but their proportions vary between pieces. A photograph of a particularly even blue example should not become a universal reference for every natural specimen. Ask whether a description refers to an actual rock identification or merely a colour resemblance. Dyed materials and imitations require clear names. Avoid assigning one refractive index to a whole aggregate simply because one constituent mineral has a familiar value."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"For beads, carvings or inlay, ask about surface treatment and the complete construction. A thin inlay bonded to another material needs to be described differently from a single carved piece. Examine edges and fittings before cleaning, and obtain advice that accounts for dye or wax where present. Do not use acid or a destructive scratch to investigate a blue object. Keep the material and treatment description with the item. Visible pyrite is an interesting feature, but its presence alone is not an authentication test or proof of a particular mine."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For lapis lazuli, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Lapis lazuli reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/lapis-lazuli","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Lapis lazuli: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore lapis lazuli, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-turquoise","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Turquoise","slug":"turquoise","summary":"Turquoise is a blue to green gem material whose appearance may include a visible matrix. Porosity and treatment affect practical care.","family":"Turquoise","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["blue green"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Turquoise"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"5–6"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Turquoise is a blue to green gem material whose appearance may include a visible matrix. Porosity and treatment affect practical care.\n\nStabilization, dye and imitation are different descriptions. A matrix pattern does not establish a mine or prove that material is untreated."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Protect against cosmetics, chemicals and heat. Obtain treatment details before cleaning and avoid assuming all turquoise can be immersed safely."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Compare an identification with the treatment description, because both matter for turquoise. Stabilized material, dyed material and an imitation are not interchangeable labels. Ask whether the actual turquoise was identified, what treatment is known and which components of a composite object were examined. A visible matrix may be part of a piece’s appearance, but it does not certify a mine. Marketing names that suggest a locality should be supported separately. Consider the written evidence before treating a pattern seen in a photograph as a geographic fingerprint."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Plan everyday handling around potential contact with cosmetics, skin products, chemicals and heat. Before cleaning a strand or mounted gem, ask about porosity, treatment and the condition of fittings or adhesives. Do not assume that a cleaner suitable for another blue stone is suitable here. A reference hardness describes scratching resistance in general, not the full durability of an individual object. Keep treatment details with the piece so that a jeweller can make an informed care decision. Where the treatment history is unknown, preserve that uncertainty in the record."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For turquoise, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Turquoise reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/turquoise","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Turquoise: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore turquoise, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-pearl","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Pearl","slug":"pearl","summary":"Pearls form in mollusks. Cultured pearls involve human intervention in their formation, but cultured does not mean imitation.","family":"Biogenic material","material":"Organic gem material","colours":["white"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Biogenic material"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"2.5–3"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Pearls form in mollusks. Cultured pearls involve human intervention in their formation, but cultured does not mean imitation.\n\nNatural versus cultured, freshwater versus saltwater and pearl type are separate descriptive questions. Shape and lustre in a photograph cannot answer all of them."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Use a soft damp cloth, protect from cosmetics and store separately from harder gems. Check stringing and fittings regularly; ask for advice about treated pearls."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Read natural or cultured before making comparisons based on shape or lustre. A cultured pearl is formed in a mollusk with human intervention; an imitation is a different product. Freshwater and saltwater descriptions address another part of the formation context. Ask which type is being described and whether any colour treatment is disclosed. A round white appearance alone does not answer these questions. For a strand, ask whether the description applies to every pearl and how any matching, treatment or grading terminology is being used."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Inspect the whole piece, including drilling, stringing, knots and fittings where relevant. The way pearls are mounted can affect cleaning and maintenance as much as the appearance of one pearl. Keep them away from harder jewellery in storage and put them on after cosmetics and perfume. A soft damp cloth is a useful general starting point, but repairs and uncertain treatments call for individual advice. If the strand is a gift, include the care instructions and the written type and treatment description rather than relying only on a decorative certificate or photograph."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For pearl, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Pearl reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/pearl","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Pearl: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore pearl, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-amber","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Amber","slug":"amber","summary":"Amber is fossilized resin and is an organic gem material rather than a mineral. Its low hardness affects handling and jewellery design.","family":"Fossil resin","material":"Organic gem material","colours":["orange"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Fossil resin"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"2–2.5"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Amber is fossilized resin and is an organic gem material rather than a mineral. Its low hardness affects handling and jewellery design.\n\nAn insect-like inclusion does not establish age or authenticity. Resin imitations, reconstituted material and treatment claims require appropriate examination."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Protect from heat, chemicals and abrasion. Do not use a hot needle, flame, solvent or other damaging home test on a specimen."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Amber belongs to an organic-material discussion rather than a mineral-family comparison. Separate fossil resin from imitations and from other resins that may resemble it. An insect or plant-like inclusion can be visually interesting without proving age, authenticity or locality. Ask which examination supports the material identification and what is known about processing. If an object is described as reconstructed or assembled, that construction should remain part of the description. Do not assume that every inclusion shown in a highly magnified photograph is equally visible in ordinary use."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"Soft surfaces and sensitivity to heat make handling a practical part of choosing amber jewellery. Ask how the item will be protected from abrasion and contact with chemicals, and inspect fittings without pulling or twisting the material. Do not carry out flame, hot-needle or solvent experiments on an object you want to preserve. A destructive test may damage both the specimen and useful evidence. Keep the description and any examination findings together. General information about fossil resin should not be turned into a specific age or source claim for an unexamined piece."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For amber, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Amber reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/amber","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":[],"seoTitle":"Amber: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore amber, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"gemstones-sunstone","content":{"kind":"gemstones","title":"Sunstone","slug":"sunstone","summary":"Sunstone is a feldspar gem associated with reflective inclusions that produce aventurescence. The feldspar material and the appearance term should both be understood.","family":"Feldspar","material":"Mineral gem material","colours":["orange"],"properties":[{"name":"Material classification","value":"Feldspar"},{"name":"Mohs hardness (reference)","value":"6.0–7.2"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Identity and appearance","body":"Sunstone is a feldspar gem associated with reflective inclusions that produce aventurescence. The feldspar material and the appearance term should both be understood.\n\nCopper inclusions are associated with Oregon sunstone, but should not be assumed for every sunstone. A glittering appearance alone does not establish locality."},{"heading":"Care and handling","body":"Ask about the identified feldspar, treatment and condition. Protect the surface from impacts and seek cleaning advice for included or fractured stones."},{"heading":"Questions for an individual stone","body":"Keep the material name, natural or laboratory-grown origin, treatment description and any report together. Ask which conclusions were established by examination and which remain unknown. The reference properties here describe a material; they are not measurements of a stone offered for sale.\n\nIf a description and report disagree, request clarification before making a decision. Photographs are useful for discussing appearance, but cannot authenticate a specimen. Keep care instructions with the item so that a jeweller or repairer can assess it before work begins."},{"heading":"Comparison and interpretation","body":"Distinguish the feldspar identification from the appearance called aventurescence. Reflective inclusions can create a sparkling impression, but their nature and the exact feldspar material should not be guessed from a small photograph. Ask for the underlying identification and the evidence behind any locality statement. Copper-related Oregon sunstone is a particular context, not a description to extend automatically to every stone sold as sunstone. A trade name may introduce an interesting effect while leaving mineral composition and origin as separate questions."},{"heading":"Choosing and documenting a piece","body":"View the effect from several directions in ordinary light. A strongly lit close-up may emphasize reflections that are less conspicuous at normal distance. Decide which appearance is important for the intended piece, then ask a jeweller about the actual stone’s condition and mounting. Inclusions and fractures can affect practical handling, so obtain cleaning advice before using mechanical or chemical methods. Keep the written disclosure with the item. A reference illustration or photograph should help explain a concept, not stand in for identification or a treatment assessment of another specimen."},{"heading":"Before making a decision","body":"For sunstone, ask the seller to identify the actual item, state what remains unknown and explain the scope of any report. Keep photographs, measurements and written disclosures together. If an answer matters to your decision, obtain it before commissioning a setting or arranging a repair."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Sunstone reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/sunstone","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["feldspar","moonstone"],"seoTitle":"Sunstone: identity, care and questions | KhyatiGems","metaDescription":"Explore sunstone, its material identity, distinguishing features, care considerations and questions to ask about an individual gemstone.","review":null,"imageIds":[]},"reviewNotes":"Original source-linked draft. Named qualified reviewer and image approval pending. Not published."},{"id":"families-quartz","content":{"kind":"families","title":"Quartz family","slug":"quartz","summary":"Quartz includes macrocrystalline varieties such as amethyst and citrine, as well as microcrystalline materials commonly described as chalcedony. A variety name describes more than a colour label, but does not establish whether colour was modified.","family":"Quartz","sections":[{"heading":"What belongs together","body":"Quartz includes macrocrystalline varieties such as amethyst and citrine, as well as microcrystalline materials commonly described as chalcedony. A variety name describes more than a colour label, but does not establish whether colour was modified."},{"heading":"How to read the names","body":"Compare the description of the actual material and its treatment. A quartz identification alone does not demonstrate that a purple or yellow colour formed naturally."},{"heading":"Explore the members","body":"Use the related material articles to compare identity and care. Keep findings about the individual gemstone separate from general family properties. A shared family does not imply equal durability, treatment history or price."},{"heading":"Use the family reference carefully","body":"Amethyst: Purple quartz belongs to the same mineral species as citrine. Colour zoning and crystal form can be interesting features, but neither establishes natural origin or treatment status.\n\nCitrine: Citrine is yellow to orange quartz. Its relationship to amethyst is mineralogical, rather than a statement that every yellow quartz crystal formed with its present colour.\n\nFamily names organize a reference library; they are not a quality ranking or a promise about the history of an individual object. When comparing members, keep mineral identity, variety, colour, formation and treatment in separate fields. The same family can include stones with very different inclusions and practical care requirements.\n\nStart with the linked member references, then ask which conclusions apply to the actual item you are discussing. A general family relationship does not establish a mine or make a photograph an identification test. Keep a report’s qualifications and limits with the material description. If a statement is unsupported, record it as unknown rather than filling the gap from the family name."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Quartz reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/amethyst","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["amethyst","citrine"],"review":null,"imageIds":[],"seoTitle":"Quartz family | KhyatiGems reference","metaDescription":"Quartz includes macrocrystalline varieties such as amethyst and citrine, as well as microcrystalline materials commonly described as chalcedony. A variety name describes more than a colour label, but does not establ"},"reviewNotes":"Draft family overview; factual review pending."},{"id":"families-feldspar","content":{"kind":"families","title":"Feldspar family","slug":"feldspar","summary":"Feldspar is a mineral group. Moonstone and sunstone are familiar gem names associated with optical effects, rather than interchangeable names for one uniform composition.","family":"Feldspar","sections":[{"heading":"What belongs together","body":"Feldspar is a mineral group. Moonstone and sunstone are familiar gem names associated with optical effects, rather than interchangeable names for one uniform composition."},{"heading":"How to read the names","body":"Adularescence is a moving sheen; aventurescence arises from reflective inclusions. Do not infer the exact feldspar species from either effect without examination."},{"heading":"Explore the members","body":"Use the related material articles to compare identity and care. Keep findings about the individual gemstone separate from general family properties. A shared family does not imply equal durability, treatment history or price."},{"heading":"Use the family reference carefully","body":"Moonstone: Moonstone belongs to the feldspar family. Its characteristic moving sheen is called adularescence and is associated with its internal structure.\n\nSunstone: Sunstone is a feldspar gem associated with reflective inclusions that produce aventurescence. The feldspar material and the appearance term should both be understood.\n\nFamily names organize a reference library; they are not a quality ranking or a promise about the history of an individual object. When comparing members, keep mineral identity, variety, colour, formation and treatment in separate fields. The same family can include stones with very different inclusions and practical care requirements.\n\nStart with the linked member references, then ask which conclusions apply to the actual item you are discussing. A general family relationship does not establish a mine or make a photograph an identification test. Keep a report’s qualifications and limits with the material description. If a statement is unsupported, record it as unknown rather than filling the gap from the family name."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Feldspar reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/moonstone","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["moonstone","sunstone"],"review":null,"imageIds":[],"seoTitle":"Feldspar family | KhyatiGems reference","metaDescription":"Feldspar is a mineral group. Moonstone and sunstone are familiar gem names associated with optical effects, rather than interchangeable names for one uniform composition."},"reviewNotes":"Draft family overview; factual review pending."},{"id":"families-garnet","content":{"kind":"families","title":"Garnet family","slug":"garnet","summary":"The garnet group includes several mineral species and compositions. Familiar examples include pyrope, almandine, spessartine, grossular and andradite; mixtures complicate simple labels.","family":"Garnet","sections":[{"heading":"What belongs together","body":"The garnet group includes several mineral species and compositions. Familiar examples include pyrope, almandine, spessartine, grossular and andradite; mixtures complicate simple labels."},{"heading":"How to read the names","body":"A group name cannot supply one universal refractive index, density or colour. Ask for the identified species or variety and the basis for the conclusion."},{"heading":"Explore the members","body":"Use the related material articles to compare identity and care. Keep findings about the individual gemstone separate from general family properties. A shared family does not imply equal durability, treatment history or price."},{"heading":"Use the family reference carefully","body":"Garnet: Garnet is a mineral group rather than one uniform species. Pyrope, almandine, spessartine, grossular and andradite are examples within its broad gem vocabulary.\n\nFamily names organize a reference library; they are not a quality ranking or a promise about the history of an individual object. When comparing members, keep mineral identity, variety, colour, formation and treatment in separate fields. The same family can include stones with very different inclusions and practical care requirements.\n\nStart with the linked member references, then ask which conclusions apply to the actual item you are discussing. A general family relationship does not establish a mine or make a photograph an identification test. Keep a report’s qualifications and limits with the material description. If a statement is unsupported, record it as unknown rather than filling the gap from the family name."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Garnet reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/garnet","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["garnet"],"review":null,"imageIds":[],"seoTitle":"Garnet family | KhyatiGems reference","metaDescription":"The garnet group includes several mineral species and compositions. Familiar examples include pyrope, almandine, spessartine, grossular and andradite; mixtures complicate simple labels."},"reviewNotes":"Draft family overview; factual review pending."},{"id":"families-tourmaline","content":{"kind":"families","title":"Tourmaline family","slug":"tourmaline","summary":"Tourmaline is a mineral group with diverse compositions. Gem trade vocabulary often emphasizes colour, including pink, green, blue and multicoloured material.","family":"Tourmaline","sections":[{"heading":"What belongs together","body":"Tourmaline is a mineral group with diverse compositions. Gem trade vocabulary often emphasizes colour, including pink, green, blue and multicoloured material."},{"heading":"How to read the names","body":"A colour name does not identify every chemical or geographical attribute. Separate species, trade colour name, treatment and any supported origin conclusion."},{"heading":"Explore the members","body":"Use the related material articles to compare identity and care. Keep findings about the individual gemstone separate from general family properties. A shared family does not imply equal durability, treatment history or price."},{"heading":"Use the family reference carefully","body":"Tourmaline: Tourmaline is a group of minerals with many colours and compositions. A single crystal may contain distinct colour zones.\n\nFamily names organize a reference library; they are not a quality ranking or a promise about the history of an individual object. When comparing members, keep mineral identity, variety, colour, formation and treatment in separate fields. The same family can include stones with very different inclusions and practical care requirements.\n\nStart with the linked member references, then ask which conclusions apply to the actual item you are discussing. A general family relationship does not establish a mine or make a photograph an identification test. Keep a report’s qualifications and limits with the material description. If a statement is unsupported, record it as unknown rather than filling the gap from the family name."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA Tourmaline reference","url":"https://www.gia.edu/tourmaline","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["tourmaline"],"review":null,"imageIds":[],"seoTitle":"Tourmaline family | KhyatiGems reference","metaDescription":"Tourmaline is a mineral group with diverse compositions. Gem trade vocabulary often emphasizes colour, including pink, green, blue and multicoloured material."},"reviewNotes":"Draft family overview; factual review pending."},{"id":"birthstones-january","content":{"kind":"birthstones","title":"January birthstones","slug":"january","summary":"Discover Garnet and explore the material behind the January birthstone tradition.","sections":[{"heading":"A personal connection to gemstones","body":"Garnet family is associated with January in the GIA birthstone reference. Birthstone lists vary across cultures and historical periods; a month association is not a mineral classification. This page introduces selected familiar options rather than claiming that every tradition uses the same list.\n\nTreat symbolic meanings as cultural associations. A birthstone does not promise a medical, financial or astrological result."},{"heading":"Choose the material, then consider the design","body":"Start with the linked gemstone references below. Look at the material identity, available treatment information and practical care before deciding on a ring, pendant or other use. The month label does not make different materials equally resistant to knocks, chemicals or scratching.\n\nIf you are choosing a gift, ask how often it will be worn, what colours the recipient enjoys and whether a protective setting would be useful. Keep the individual description and report with the gift. A stylized illustration shows a colour theme rather than the colour range or optical properties of every specimen."},{"heading":"Make the month personal","body":"A january birthstone can be a starting point for a personal gift without dictating the recipient’s taste. Compare the linked materials in ordinary lighting and consider comfort, intended wear and maintenance. Where more than one option is listed, the month association does not rank one above another. Choose with a clear material description and care record, rather than expecting symbolic tradition to establish authenticity or a guaranteed benefit."}],"sources":[{"title":"GIA birthstones by month","url":"https://www.gia.edu/birthstones","checkedAt":"2026-10-15"}],"relatedSlugs":["garnet"],"review":null,"imageIds":[],"seoTitle":"January birthstones | KhyatiGems reference","metaDescription":"Discover Garnet and explore the material behind the January birthstone tradition."},"reviewNotes":"Draft cultural reference; review pending. Selected options are not an exhaustive global birthstone list."},{"id":"birthstones-february","content":{"kind":"birthstones","title":"February birthstones","slug":"february","summary":"Discover Amethyst and explore the material behind the February birthstone tradition.","sections":[{"heading":"A personal connection to gemstones","body":"Amethyst is associated with February in the GIA birthstone reference. Birthstone lists vary across cultures and historical periods; a month association is not a mineral classification. This page introduces selected familiar options rather than claiming that every tradition uses the same list.\n\nTreat symbolic meanings as cultural associations. A birthstone does not promise a medical, financial or astrological result."},{"heading":"Choose the material, then consider the design","body":"Start with the linked gemstone references below. Look at the material identity, available treatment information and practical care before deciding on a ring, pendant or other use. The month label does not make different materials equally resistant to knocks, chemicals or scratching.\n\nIf you are choosing a gift, ask how often it will be worn, what colours the recipient enjoys and whether 