iGems inventory and not an independent treatment or origin assessment.","sourceUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jadeite_(GeoDIL_number_-_1607).jpg","creator":"Shannon Heinle","license":"CC0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","attribution":"Shannon Heinle · CC0","commercialUse":true,"modifications":"Displayed at responsive sizes; original source linked. Any adapted image remains under the source licence.","specimen":"Jade","verifiedAt":"2026-10-16","permissionEvidence":"Licence and author checked at the original Commons file page on 2026-10-16. CC0; commercial reuse permitted under linked terms. Source-labelled specimen, not independently authenticated.","authenticPhoto":true,"status":"approved","cloudinaryPublicId":"khyatigems-website/reference/2026-10-16/jade"},"review":{"method":"AI-assisted metadata and visual review","sourceDescription":"Jadeite is one of the two minerals called jade. It is a member of the pyroxene series. It has the chemical formula of NaAlSi2O6. Jadeite has been a mystery to geologists for centuries because they could not determine its source. Jadeite is now known to form in only high pressure and temperature regimes that are coincident with continent to continent collisions. Some countries it can be found in are Burma, Yunan, China, and Japan. This sample is approximately 4 cm.","authority":"Owner delegation, 16 October 2026","scope":"Reference photography only; no independent authentication of specimen treatment or origin.","delivery":"Uploaded to ERP-configured Cloudinary; original Commons attribution and licence retained."}},{"slug":"lapis-lazuli","id":"commons-lapis-lazuli","asset":{"title":"Lapis Lazuli at Natural History Museum Washington D.C.jpg","url":"https://res.cloudinary.com/dxu1jawbs/image/upload/v1792155191/khyatigems-website/reference/2026-10-16/lapis-lazuli.jpg","width":960,"height":1280,"alt":"Lapis lazuli reference photograph, identified by the source.","caption":"Lapis laz